1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,694
A peepal leaf pressed in a notebook.

2
00:00:02,694 --> 00:00:04,193
A long blade of grass.

3
00:00:04,193 --> 00:00:06,156
A pea plant gripping the fence.

4
00:00:06,556 --> 00:00:07,952
All three are leaves.

5
00:00:07,952 --> 00:00:12,303
But look closely at the lines inside
them, and at the way they hold on.

6
00:00:12,753 --> 00:00:16,794
Diya asked: why does one leaf have
a net of lines, another has straight

7
00:00:16,794 --> 00:00:18,989
lines, and one turns into a spring?

8
00:00:19,339 --> 00:00:23,169
Because leaves follow patterns: in
their veins, in how they sit on the

9
00:00:23,169 --> 00:00:25,350
stem, and in the jobs they take on.

10
00:00:25,850 --> 00:00:29,648
By the end, you will read a leaf's
parts, its venation, its phyllotaxy,

11
00:00:29,648 --> 00:00:31,952
and every leaf modification
exams ask about.

12
00:00:32,602 --> 00:00:37,442
First, a leaf grows at a node of the
stem, and it has a bud in its axil,

13
00:00:37,442 --> 00:00:39,174
the angle above the leaf.

14
00:00:39,624 --> 00:00:41,691
A typical leaf has three parts.

15
00:00:41,691 --> 00:00:44,064
The leaf base joins it to the stem.

16
00:00:44,064 --> 00:00:47,403
It may carry two small
leaf-like parts called stipules.

17
00:00:48,003 --> 00:00:49,866
The petiole is the leaf stalk.

18
00:00:49,866 --> 00:00:52,101
It holds the blade out to the light.

19
00:00:52,701 --> 00:00:55,004
The lamina is the flat green blade.

20
00:00:55,004 --> 00:00:59,220
A thick midrib runs down its middle,
and veins branch out from it.

21
00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:04,135
The veins make the blade stiff,
and carry water, minerals and food.

22
00:01:04,785 --> 00:01:06,877
Now look closely at those veins.

23
00:01:07,227 --> 00:01:10,694
The way veins and veinlets are arranged
in the lamina is called venation.

24
00:01:11,294 --> 00:01:15,919
In a peepal leaf, the veinlets join
up into a network, like a fishing net.

25
00:01:15,919 --> 00:01:17,650
This is reticulate venation.

26
00:01:18,400 --> 00:01:22,871
Zoom in, and you see tiny islands of
green, each one closed in by veinlets.

27
00:01:23,621 --> 00:01:27,981
In a grass leaf, the veins run
side by side, like railway lines.

28
00:01:27,981 --> 00:01:29,743
This is parallel venation.

29
00:01:30,493 --> 00:01:33,352
Leaves of dicots usually
have reticulate venation.

30
00:01:33,352 --> 00:01:35,517
Most monocots have parallel venation.

31
00:01:36,167 --> 00:01:37,111
Next, phyllotaxy.

32
00:01:37,111 --> 00:01:38,061
Phyllo means leaf.

33
00:01:38,061 --> 00:01:41,076
Phyllotaxy is the pattern in which
leaves are arranged on a stem.

34
00:01:41,076 --> 00:01:42,213
There are three types.

35
00:01:42,713 --> 00:01:43,613
Alternate.

36
00:01:43,496 --> 00:01:48,136
A single leaf grows at each node,
first on one side, then on the other,

37
00:01:48,136 --> 00:01:50,814
as in china rose, mustard and sunflower.

38
00:01:51,564 --> 00:01:52,464
Opposite.

39
00:01:52,173 --> 00:01:55,933
A pair of leaves grows at each node,
facing each other, as in Calotropis

40
00:01:55,933 --> 00:01:56,833
and guava.

41
00:01:57,380 --> 00:01:58,280
Whorled.

42
00:01:57,964 --> 00:02:01,696
More than two leaves grow at one
node, in a ring, as in Alstonia.

43
00:02:02,446 --> 00:02:04,901
Finally, leaves that take on new jobs.

44
00:02:05,251 --> 00:02:06,151
Climbing.

45
00:02:05,864 --> 00:02:09,180
In peas, leaves turn into tendrils
that coil around a support.

46
00:02:09,930 --> 00:02:10,830
Defence.

47
00:02:10,565 --> 00:02:14,604
In cacti, leaves turn into spines,
so animals cannot eat the plant.

48
00:02:14,604 --> 00:02:16,901
The green stem makes the food instead.

49
00:02:17,651 --> 00:02:18,646
Storing food.

50
00:02:18,646 --> 00:02:22,615
The fleshy leaves of onion and
garlic are full of stored food.

51
00:02:23,365 --> 00:02:26,938
In Australian acacia, the
leaves are small and fall early.

52
00:02:26,938 --> 00:02:30,558
The petiole spreads out, turns
green, and makes the food.

53
00:02:31,308 --> 00:02:32,263
Catching insects.

54
00:02:32,263 --> 00:02:35,932
The pitcher of the pitcher plant, and
the trap of the Venus flytrap, are

55
00:02:35,932 --> 00:02:37,051
modified leaves too.

56
00:02:37,801 --> 00:02:40,989
Now the two venation types, side by side.

57
00:02:41,339 --> 00:02:43,755
Both carry water, minerals and food.

58
00:02:43,755 --> 00:02:45,536
Both make the blade stiff.

59
00:02:45,986 --> 00:02:49,922
But reticulate veins form a
net, as in peepal, a dicot.

60
00:02:49,922 --> 00:02:53,909
Parallel veins run side by
side, as in grass, a monocot.

61
00:02:54,559 --> 00:02:55,459
Your turn.

62
00:02:55,406 --> 00:02:59,177
A leaf has veins running side
by side, from base to tip.

63
00:02:59,177 --> 00:03:02,392
Is the plant more likely
a monocot, or a dicot?

64
00:03:02,392 --> 00:03:03,639
Pause and decide.

65
00:03:06,839 --> 00:03:07,739
A monocot!

66
00:03:07,590 --> 00:03:11,396
Parallel venation is typical of
most monocots, like grass and wheat.

67
00:03:12,046 --> 00:03:12,976
Remember this.

68
00:03:12,976 --> 00:03:14,940
Venation is the pattern of veins.

69
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:17,713
Phyllotaxy is the pattern
of leaves on the stem.

70
00:03:18,313 --> 00:03:19,955
Now, a worked example.

71
00:03:19,955 --> 00:03:21,500
Let's read four plants.

72
00:03:21,850 --> 00:03:24,580
Guava, two leaves at each
node, facing each other.

73
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:26,058
That is opposite phyllotaxy.

74
00:03:26,508 --> 00:03:28,446
Alstonia, a ring of leaves at one node.

75
00:03:28,446 --> 00:03:29,729
That is whorled phyllotaxy.

76
00:03:30,179 --> 00:03:32,190
Onion, with thick juicy layers.

77
00:03:32,190 --> 00:03:34,439
Those are fleshy leaves storing food.

78
00:03:34,889 --> 00:03:38,679
Australian acacia, with a flat
green petiole making food.

79
00:03:38,679 --> 00:03:42,186
That is a modified petiole
doing the job of a leaf.

80
00:03:42,836 --> 00:03:44,300
Now, the exam trap.

81
00:03:44,650 --> 00:03:48,602
Many students think the pea tendril
and the cucumber tendril are the same

82
00:03:48,602 --> 00:03:50,225
thing, because both coil.

83
00:03:50,625 --> 00:03:53,427
But the pea tendril is a modified leaf.

84
00:03:53,427 --> 00:03:58,102
The cucumber tendril is a modified
stem, grown from an axillary bud.

85
00:03:58,752 --> 00:04:00,080
Where is this asked?

86
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:04,661
As matching a plant with its phyllotaxy,
or a plant with its leaf modification.

87
00:04:05,261 --> 00:04:06,420
Exam level now.

88
00:04:06,420 --> 00:04:08,540
Match each type with its plant.

89
00:04:08,540 --> 00:04:09,748
Pause and solve.

90
00:04:15,148 --> 00:04:16,564
The answer is C.

91
00:04:16,564 --> 00:04:19,341
A four, B three, C one, D two.

92
00:04:19,841 --> 00:04:23,915
Mustard is alternate, guava is
opposite, and Alstonia is whorled.

93
00:04:24,415 --> 00:04:28,231
And in peas, the leaf is modified
into a tendril for climbing.

94
00:04:28,831 --> 00:04:30,371
Here is your recap.

95
00:04:30,371 --> 00:04:31,954
Screenshot this card.

96
00:04:35,154 --> 00:04:37,340
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

97
00:04:37,740 --> 00:04:38,640
One.

98
00:04:38,214 --> 00:04:40,702
Name the three parts of a typical leaf.

99
00:04:41,602 --> 00:04:42,502
Two.

100
00:04:41,866 --> 00:04:43,356
Which type of phyllotaxy
does Alstonia show?

101
00:04:44,256 --> 00:04:45,156
Three.

102
00:04:44,816 --> 00:04:47,785
In Australian acacia,
which part makes the food?

103
00:04:48,885 --> 00:04:53,437
Next time: on a mustard stalk, the
oldest flowers sit at the bottom.

104
00:04:53,437 --> 00:04:54,337
Why?

105
00:04:53,938 --> 00:04:57,212
We find out in inflorescence:
racemose and cymose.
